# November 28, 2026: a first scatter across the parts of Newfoundland and Labrador the earlier batches did not reach:
# the Labrador coast (Battle Harbour, Hopedale, Hebron), the Great Northern Peninsula and Gros Morne (L'Anse aux Meadows,
# Lobster Cove Head), the south coast (Rose Blanche), Corner Brook, Notre Dame Bay (Twillingate, Fogo Island, Tilting),
# Greenspond, and the southern Avalon (Ferryland, Cape Race, Placentia). Region nl drawn by tools/choose.py --thinnest.
# Sources opened: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for each place (historicplaces.ca); Parks Canada,
# Battle Harbour, Colony of Avalon and Town of Fogo Island pages as quoted by web search results (hours); Wikipedia's lists of
# historic places by region (raw text) for the Register ids and positions.
from data_b import N
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'nl_hzqd','label':'Across Newfoundland and Labrador: the Labrador coast, Gros Morne, Notre Dame Bay, the south coast and the southern Avalon','added':'2026-11-28 15:38'}
PLACES=[
N('battle-harbour-labrador','Battle Harbour Historic District','Battle Harbour','nl','town','standing',52.274,-55.5854,'area','open','boat','','1770s; buildings 1834–1950',
  "A fishing and trading outport on an island off the southeast coast of Labrador. John Slade of Poole, England, founded its merchant premises in the 1770s, and it was called the capital of Labrador up to the 1960s (Register). The large wooden Salt, Flour and Salmon Stores, the wharves and fishing stages, a Gothic Revival church (St. James the Apostle), a Grenfell Mission doctor's cottage and police buildings still stand, with fishing rooms that are used seasonally. It is a National Historic Site of Canada (designated 1997).",
  "The mercantile complex of big gable-roofed timber-framed stores round the wharf, with houses, the church and the police buildings climbing the slope above; the cemeteries on the island and the remains of fish stages on neighbouring Great Caribou and Gunning islands are part of the designation (Register).",
  "A whole merchant outport of wooden stores, left as it was when it was Labrador's capital.",
  "The site's own page says it is reached by passenger ferry from Mary's Harbour, one crossing a day at 11 a.m. (arrive at its visitor centre by 10:30), about an hour in an open boat; booking is required, luggage is limited to one bag of 30 pounds, vehicles are not carried, and its season in 2025 was June 9 to September 2 (as quoted by a web search result; 2026 dates not checked). It also lets rooms. Nobody should land without arranging it.",
  [HP('Battle Harbour Historic District',3453),L('Battle Harbour: getting here','https://battleharbour.com/getting-here/')],brick='no',bcol='none',hours='Season about June to early September; daily ferry from Mary\'s Harbour at 11 a.m., booking required (2025 dates)'),
N('hopedale-mission-labrador','Hopedale Mission','Hopedale','nl','worship','standing',55.45877,-60.21244,'site','open','boat','','1782; buildings 1850s–1892',
  "The Moravian Church founded its mission at Hopedale, on the Labrador coast, in 1782. The surviving complex holds seven buildings: the early mission building, the Mission House (two wings), the church, a link between them (all finished by 1850 to 1861), the Reserve Storehouse (1892) and the Dead House (1861); an oil and salt storehouse was pulled down in 1999 (Register). It stands for the dealings between the Labrador Inuit and the missionaries, and it is a National Historic Site of Canada (designated 1970).",
  "Large wooden buildings of varied heights, covered in lime-washed clapboard with shake roofs and, in places, brick in-filling between the timbers, grouped tightly on the rocky shore; the remains of earlier buildings, a garden and the blubber yard lie in the ground between (Register).",
  "Moravian mission buildings of the 1850s standing starkly on a bare Labrador shore.",
  "No road reaches Hopedale (as quoted by a web search result); how to get there was not checked. Parks Canada's page lists the site's hours as always open, which can only mean the grounds, and a 2019 magazine article says tours of the buildings are offered at the Hopedale Mission Complex and Interpretation Centre; no tour times were found.",
  [HP('Hopedale Mission',11597),L('Parks Canada: Hopedale Mission National Historic Site','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nl/hopedalemission')],brick='?',hours='Grounds always open; tour times not found'),
N('hebron-mission-labrador','Hebron Mission','Hebron','nl','worship','ruins',58.199,-62.6273,'site','view','boat','','1829–1837; closed 1959',
  "A Moravian mission on the far northeast coast of Labrador. Building began in 1829 and the place was ready to live in by 1837; the Moravians taught, traded, ran a clinic and administered justice, and the mission closed in 1959 (Register). What is left is a long interconnected run of buildings, a church, a mission house and a store, with a steep elongated roof, small dormers and a cupola; the forge, the carpenter's shop and the other buildings are gone. The Register says the buildings may have been prefabricated in Germany. National Historic Site of Canada, designated 1976.",
  "The church-mission house-store range with its Germanic roof and cupola (Register). A 2015 news piece quoted by a web search says descendants have been restoring the buildings and show visitors round in summer; that was not checked for later years.",
  "A Moravian mission range, shut in 1959, at the edge of the Torngat Mountains.",
  "It lies on the coast at the edge of Torngat Mountains National Park, reached only by boat, charter plane or helicopter in summer (Parks Canada, as quoted by a web search result). Visitors to the park must register and attend an orientation; Parks Canada's Nain office handles questions (1-709-922-1290). Whether the mission itself falls inside the park boundary was not settled (sources differ). View only unless arranged.",
  [HP('Hebron Mission',9620),L('Parks Canada: Torngat Mountains National Park','https://www.parks.canada.ca/pn-np/nl/torngats')],brick='?'),
N('lanse-aux-meadows','L\'Anse aux Meadows','L\'Anse aux Meadows','nl','town','traces',51.5984,-55.5618,'area','open','drive','','about 1000 AD',
  "The archaeological remains of a Viking base on the east shore of Epaves Bay, at the tip of the Great Northern Peninsula, 1 km south of the village: the first known site established by Vikings in North America and the earliest evidence of Europeans in Canada (Register). It was a short-lived exploration base, popularly linked with Leif Ericsson's Vinland camp. National Historic Site of Canada.",
  "The low grassed outlines of the Norse buildings, with a partial reconstruction of the habitation built by Parks Canada for visitors (Register); a visitor centre and a costumed Viking encampment (Parks Canada, as quoted by a web search result).",
  "The only certain Viking settlement in North America, a thousand years old.",
  "Run by Parks Canada. Its page, as quoted by a web search result, gives 2026 hours for the visitor centre and the Viking encampment of June 1 to October 2, daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (admission fees not checked; telephone 709-623-2608 in season). Parking is at the visitor centre (not checked).",
  [HP("L'Anse aux Meadows",4219),L("Parks Canada: L'Anse aux Meadows hours",'https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nl/meadows/visit/heures-hours')],brick='no',bcol='none',hours='June 1 to October 2, 2026, daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('lobster-cove-head-lighthouse','Lobster Cove Head Lighthouse','Rocky Harbour','nl','landmark','standing',49.6029,-57.9556,'site','open','drive','','1897',
  "A white, straight-sided cast-iron lighthouse with a domed lantern on a rocky outcrop on Newfoundland's west coast, built in 1897 and a Recognized Federal Heritage Building. The Register calls it a good example of a prefabricated tower of about 1900, its shaft cast locally, and says its building showed the colony finally accepting responsibility for safe navigation on the west coast. The lightkeeper's house and a shed stand with it.",
  "The cast-iron tower and the keeper's house, now an exhibit run by Gros Morne National Park (Parks Canada, as quoted by a web search result); the interior was not checked.",
  "A cast-iron lighthouse of 1897 on a rock above the Gulf.",
  "Gros Morne National Park: its page, as quoted by a web search result, gives 2026 hours for the lighthouse exhibit of May 22 to October 12, 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. daily (the French page gave May 15), with the grounds open all year. Park admission and parking were not checked.",
  [HP('Lobster Cove Head Lighthouse',4813),L('Parks Canada: Lobster Cove Head Lighthouse','https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/nl/grosmorne/activ/decouverte-tours/lch')],brick='no',bcol='none',hours='Exhibit May 22 to October 12, 2026, daily 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m.; grounds open all year'),
N('rose-blanche-lighthouse','Rose Blanche Lighthouse','Rose Blanche','nl','landmark','standing',47.60188,-58.69466,'site','open','drive','','1871–1873',
  "A large granite lighthouse and keeper's house on a rocky cliff at Rose Blanche, on the southwest coast, one of the only granite lighthouses left in Newfoundland. The granite came from a quarry just below the site; the firm D. and T. Stevenson designed it, local residents built it in 1871 and it began working in 1873 (Register). It is now a registered heritage structure of the province, and the Fresnel lens on display is a gift of the Canadian Coast Guard.",
  "Granite walls on a stone foundation, multi-paned wooden windows, a slate roof, and inside a stone staircase and old woodwork; the tower holds a small sixth-order Fresnel lens (Register).",
  "One of the last granite lighthouses in Newfoundland, built by the people of the village.",
  "A provincial tourism listing, as quoted by a web search result, gives a season of June 9 to September 13 and a fee (a map listing gave $7 for an adult; its daily hours of 9 a.m. to 9 p.m. came from that map listing, so they were not relied on). Who owns it and where to park were not checked.",
  [HP('Rose Blanche Lighthouse',2095)],arch='D and T Stevenson',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places (the firm that designed it)',builder='local residents',brick='no',bcol='none',hours='Season about June 9 to September 13; daily hours not confirmed'),
N('glynmill-inn-corner-brook','Glynmill Inn','Corner Brook','nl','landmark','standing',48.94928,-57.9401,'site','open','drive','','1923; hotel from 1925',
  "A four-and-a-half-storey Tudor Revival building by the Halifax architect Andrew Cobb, put up by the English firm Armstrong-Whitworth in 1923 as quarters for the senior staff building the Corner Brook pulp and paper mill, and a hotel from 1925 (Register). It is named for Sir Glyn West, the firm's chairman. The Register calls it the finest Tudor Revival building on the west coast and probably in the province; Cobb also designed about 175 houses in the Townsite area of the city.",
  "The exposed half-timbering outside is well preserved, as is much of the Arts and Crafts decoration inside (Register). It still takes guests, and clubs such as the Rotary and the Girl Guides have long met there.",
  "A half-timbered mill-town inn of 1923, still taking guests.",
  "A working hotel on a hill above a pond; walk in as any visitor to its lobby or restaurant. Its own page was not read for hours.",
  [HP('Glynmill Inn',8579)],arch='Andrew Cobb',builder='Armstrong-Whitworth Limited',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('long-point-lighthouse-twillingate','Long Point Lightstation','Twillingate','nl','landmark','standing',49.6878,-54.8002,'site','open','drive','','1876 lighthouse; present tower 1884',
  "A square-based octagonal lighthouse at Long Point, on the northeast coast, with a many-sided lantern base and two rows of triangular panes in the lantern. The Register describes it as a concrete brick tower of 1883 and rare in having only one other like it in Canada. The lightstation was set up late to support the sealing trade and has long drawn visitors.",
  "The tower, with a double dwelling and other station buildings, set on headland cliffs; a tourism listing says there is a historical exhibit and tower climbs of three people at a time (the dates the sources give differ: 1876 and 1883).",
  "An eight-sided Newfoundland lighthouse on a cliff of whales and icebergs.",
  "A provincial tourism listing, as quoted by a web search result, gives daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. with the last tour at 4:45, $5 for the exhibit or $10 with the tower climb; no season dates were found and it is probably closed for winter (not checked). It is on Lighthouse Road in Crow Head.",
  [HP('Long Point Lighthouse',9675)],brick='?',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. in season (season dates not found)'),
N('bleak-house-fogo','Bleak House','Fogo','nl','landmark','standing',49.71801,-54.28507,'site','open','drive','','about 1826–1827',
  "A two-storey wooden merchant's house on a small hill above Fogo, built about 1826 to 1827 for the merchant John Slade. It later belonged to his former bookkeeper John Owens and from 1897 to Henry Earle, whose firm Earle and Company traded at Fogo and Twillingate until the fishery collapsed in 1967 (Register). It has a steep gable roof, a pedimented two-storey centre bay and clapboard laid on the bias, in a style of West Country England. A caption on a Wikimedia Commons photograph says John King built it; the Register's account, with Slade, is the one given here.",
  "The house is a museum open to the public since 1988 (Museums NL, via a web search result); what is inside was not checked.",
  "The house of the Fogo merchants, on a hill above the harbour.",
  "The Town of Fogo Island's page gives 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. seven days a week, June to September, $5 a building (undated); the Fogo Island Inn says museum hours vary week to week. Parking was not checked.",
  [HP('Bleak House',1891),L('Town of Fogo Island: attractions','https://townoffogoisland.ca/home/attractions.htm')],brick='no',bcol='none',hours='June to September, 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. daily (the town\'s page is undated)'),
N('tilting-fogo-island','Tilting','Tilting','nl','town','standing',49.7081,-54.0653,'area','view','drive','','1730s onward',
  "An outport on the east coast of Fogo Island where Irish settlers began to shape the land in the 1730s. The Register says it keeps almost the whole range of traditional buildings and spaces of the inshore fishery in their old relation, with family clusters round the harbour and fenced gardens, and that its landscape has been used in much the same way since at least the mid-1700s. National Historic Site of Canada, designated 2002.",
  "A church, parish hall, former school, post office, two stores, houses and fishing buildings (stages, stables, barns, root cellars), three cemeteries, gardens and fences, with Pigeon Island across the harbour (Register).",
  "An Irish outport still worked the old way, fences and all.",
  "A living village: look from the road and the harbour and respect the private houses and gardens. Parking and any visitor centre were not checked.",
  [HP('Tilting Registered Heritage District',13153)],brick='?'),
N('greenspond-courthouse','Greenspond Courthouse','Greenspond','nl','civic','standing',49.0685,-53.56687,'site','view','drive','','1899–1901',
  "A wooden two-storey Second Empire courthouse with a mansard roof and a domed tower, on a hill above Greenspond harbour, designed by William Henry Churchill, Superintendent of Public Buildings. Its cornerstone was laid in 1899 and building went on for two years. It served as courthouse and home of the jailer and his family, and as a morgue after shipwrecks; the Register says the courtroom and the living quarters upstairs are well preserved and it is one of few early 20th-century courthouses left in the province.",
  "Mansard roof, pedimented dormers, eave brackets, a tower topped with a dome and cresting, and period doors, stairs and wainscoting inside (Register). Its present use was not checked.",
  "A wooden Second Empire courthouse with a dome, over a Bonavista Bay harbour.",
  "Look from the street; whether the interior opens was not checked. Parking was not checked.",
  [HP('Greenspond Courthouse',2074)],arch='William Henry Churchill',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places',brick='no',bcol='none'),
N('colony-of-avalon-ferryland','Colony of Avalon, Ferryland','Ferryland','nl','town','traces',47.0227,-52.88,'area','open','drive','','1621–1696',
  "The site of the English settlement founded in 1621 by Sir George Calvert (later Lord Baltimore), who lived at the Mansion House in 1628 and left the colony in 1629; Sir David Kirke took it over and governed Newfoundland from it from 1637 to 1650. The French burned it in 1696. Digging by the Colony of Avalon Foundation has found much of the 17th-century settlement and many objects (Register). National Historic Site of Canada, designated 1953.",
  "A working archaeological dig with a conservation lab, a visitor centre, a reconstructed 17th-century kitchen and gardens (the foundation's page, as quoted by a web search result).",
  "A 1620s English colony that is still being dug up.",
  "Run by the Colony of Avalon Foundation. Its page gives a June to October 2026 season, with guided tours several times a day (its pages disagree on the times, and give adult admission as $20), a cafe and parking. It was closed for the season when read.",
  [HP('Colony of Avalon',19508),L('Colony of Avalon','https://colonyofavalon.ca/')],brick='?',hours='June to October 2026, guided tours several times a day (times differ between its pages)'),
N('castle-hill-placentia','Castle Hill National Historic Site','Placentia','nl','landmark','ruins',47.25104,-53.97168,'site','open','drive','','1692–1811',
  "The remains of French and British forts on a headland high above Placentia: defensive walls, three military works, a British blockhouse and a historic path, in use from 1692 to 1811 by France (to 1713) and then Britain (Register). Parks Canada made it a historic park in 1962 to 1968 for Placentia's 300th year, with digging at that time.",
  "Earthworks and walls on the hilltop with a lookout and picnic area, and a Parks Canada visitor centre (Parks Canada).",
  "French and English fort walls above Placentia's two arms of the sea.",
  "Parks Canada: its page, as quoted by a web search result, gives 2026 hours for the visitor centre of June 1 to October 9, daily 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., admission fees apply; parking, restrooms and a gift shop are listed. Plan two to three hours (Parks Canada).",
  [HP('Castle Hill',7565),L('Parks Canada: Castle Hill, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/nl/castlehill/visit')],brick='?',hours='Visitor centre June 1 to October 9, 2026, daily 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.'),
N('cape-race-lighthouse','Cape Race Lighthouse','Cape Race','nl','landmark','standing',46.6586,-53.07364,'site','view','drive','','1906–1907',
  "A plain concrete cylindrical tower with a large prefabricated metal lantern on a headland at the southeast tip of the Avalon Peninsula, the first lighthouse in Canada built of reinforced concrete and possibly among the first in the world. It was built by the Steel Concrete Co. Ltd. as the prototype of a standard design repeated elsewhere, and is a Recognized Federal Heritage Building. The Register calls it Canada's most prominent landfall marker, the first and last light seen by ships crossing the Atlantic.",
  "The tower is still a working light, tended by keepers (a web search result). A seasonal interpretation centre for the Cape Race wireless station stands nearby; its hours were not found.",
  "The first concrete lighthouse in Canada, at the first landfall on the Atlantic crossing.",
  "A working station: whether visitors may go in, the season and the road were not checked; look from outside the station.",
  [HP('Cape Race Lighthouse',3627)],builder='Steel Concrete Co. Ltd.',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places',brick='no',bcol='none'),
]
